   "text": "A curve has equation $y = (2x+1)^3\\,\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$.\n\nShow that $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 3(2x+1)^2(1-2x)\\,\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$. (4)",
   "mark_scheme": [
    {
     "code": "M1",
     "for": "Uses the product rule on $(2x+1)^3$ and $\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$: an expression of the form $\\alpha(2x+1)^2\\mathrm{e}^{-3x} \\pm \\beta(2x+1)^3\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$ with $\\alpha, \\beta$ non-zero constants",
     "notes": "Terms may be in either order. The product-rule structure (two terms, each keeping one factor unchanged) is what earns this mark, even if the constants are wrong."
    },
    {
     "code": "A1",
     "for": "Fully correct derivative before simplifying: $6(2x+1)^2\\mathrm{e}^{-3x} - 3(2x+1)^3\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$",
     "notes": "Accept $3(2x+1)^2\\times 2\\,\\mathrm{e}^{-3x} + (2x+1)^3\\times(-3)\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$ oe."
    },
    {
     "code": "M1",
     "for": "Takes $(2x+1)^2\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$ (or $3(2x+1)^2\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$) out of both terms and simplifies what remains in the bracket to a linear expression in $x$",
     "depends_on": "M1",
     "notes": "e.g. $3(2x+1)^2\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}[2-(2x+1)]$. If a student expands $(2x+1)^3$ fully instead, award this mark only if they later refactorise correctly."
    },
    {
     "code": "A1*",
     "for": "Arrives at $3(2x+1)^2(1-2x)\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$ with the bracket $2-(2x+1)=1-2x$ shown and no sign or coefficient errors at any stage",
     "notes": "cso. The step from $[2-(2x+1)]$ or $[2-2x-1]$ to $(1-2x)$ should be visible."
    }
   ],
   "solution": [
    {
     "step": 1,
     "working": "Let $u=(2x+1)^3$ and $v=\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$. By the chain rule, $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x}=3(2x+1)^2\\times 2 = 6(2x+1)^2$ and $\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x} = -3\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$.",
     "mark": "0"
    },
    {
     "step": 2,
     "working": "$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = v\\frac{\\mathrm{d}u}{\\mathrm{d}x} + u\\frac{\\mathrm{d}v}{\\mathrm{d}x}$",
     "mark": "M1"
    },
    {
     "step": 3,
     "working": "$\\frac{\\mathrm{d}y}{\\mathrm{d}x} = 6(2x+1)^2\\mathrm{e}^{-3x} - 3(2x+1)^3\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$",
     "mark": "A1"
    },
    {
     "step": 4,
     "working": "$= 3(2x+1)^2\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}\\left[2 - (2x+1)\\right] = 3(2x+1)^2\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}(2-2x-1)$",
     "mark": "M1"
    },
    {
     "step": 5,
     "working": "$= 3(2x+1)^2(1-2x)\\,\\mathrm{e}^{-3x}$ as required.",
     "mark": "A1*"
    }
   ],
